{"type": "say", "text": "Thank you both. Jordan, I really appreciate you being upfront about the allergy — that changes things, and I absolutely want you safe. Your offer to handle labeling, prepping the storage area, and carrying things to and from the back door is perfect — that's genuinely helpful and keeps you well clear of the hive. Rosa, thank you for stepping up for the hands-on work. To answer your questions: I'm thinking we harvest in the late morning, around 10 AM, when the bees are calmer and more of the foragers are out. As for quantity — the hive produced about 18 jars this season, and I want to leave a good portion for the colony to get through the leaner months. I'm thinking we take 12 jars and leave 6 for the colony. That gives us enough to share — Jordan, you'll get your jar, Rosa, you'll get yours, and the rest goes to the family pantry. Here's the plan: Rosa and I suit up with the veils and work the hive together, extracting the honey frames. We bring them to the back door, and Jordan takes over from there — labeling, sealing, and organizing the jars in the storage area. Rosa, you'd then handle the final sealing and storing once the extraction's done. Does that work for everyone before we head out?"}
```

